---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const regCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Register: the right key for the room', note: 'Same language, different keys: formal (job interview), casual (friends), professional jargon (workplace), intimate (family). Choosing the wrong register — too stiff or too street — signals outsider status faster than grammar errors do.' },
    { math: '2 · Idiom: the non-literal layer', note: '"Kick the bucket", "piece of cake", "spill the beans" — idioms are fixed phrases whose meanings can\'t be assembled from parts. They must be learned as whole units (chunks — the grammar lesson\'s logic), and they\'re everywhere: an estimated 25,000+ idioms in English.' },
    { math: '3 · Slang: the moving frontier', note: 'Slang marks in-group identity and changes fastest (generational turnover). The foreigner\'s dilemma: using slang wrongly reads as trying too hard; using none reads as distant. Learn to RECOGNISE slang before producing it.' },
    { math: '4 · Humor is the final exam', note: 'Puns, irony and sarcasm depend on pragmatics (the linguistics lesson) plus cultural knowledge. When you can make a native speaker laugh — deliberately — you\'ve crossed into real sociolinguistic competence.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/advanced/register-idiom-and-slang">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Textbook language is real but incomplete: native speakers navigate <strong>registers, idioms
    and slang</strong> — the social layers that signal belonging. This is the difference between
    being understood and being one of them, and it is learned the way native speakers learned
    it: in context, by ear, with attention to who says what to whom.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="register, idiom, slang, humour — the four social layers" widget="equationStep" config={regCfg} />

  <h2>How to acquire the social layers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consume the real register you target:</strong> workplace shows for professional register, sitcoms for casual, Twitter/forums for internet slang. Input (the listening lesson) remains the engine — now pointed at specific registers.</li>
    <li><strong>Notice register markers:</strong> contractions, fillers ("like", "vous" vs "tu"), sentence length. Train with register-switching exercises: say the same thing to a judge, a friend and a child.</li>
    <li><strong>Idioms in context, not lists:</strong> idioms learned from lists are misused hilariously; learned from shows and conversations they arrive with their social settings attached.</li>
    <li><strong>Slang has a shelf life:</strong> by the time a dictionary prints it, young speakers may have moved on. In-the-wild input (current media, live conversation) is the only reliable source — slang is a subscription, not a download.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Halliday (register theory); Coulmas (sociolinguistics); Moon, Fixed Expressions and Idioms in English.</p>
</TopicPage>
